// A city, in its owner's words: the file a repository carries to say what it is.
//
//   <repository root>/patrick-world.json
//
// GitHub can report that work happened. It cannot report what the work was FOR. So the
// GitHub transform guesses: a directory stands in for a feature, a keyword picks the region,
// a quiet spell reads as "working", and nothing is ever planned or connected because intent
// and connections leave no trace in a commit. This file is where those guesses are replaced
// by what the owner says:
//
//   - which features there are, and which paths hold each one's work
//   - what is planned and has no code yet
//   - which country the city stands in
//   - which other cities it connects to, and what flows between them
//
// It declares what exists and what is meant. It does NOT declare how much work was done:
// commits on a feature's paths still decide its storeys, its heat and its dates. Where the
// file's claim about a stage and the evidence disagree, `settleStage` below decides.
//
// Repositories never read each other. Each carries its own file; the sync reads all of them;
// a link names another city by the `id` in THAT city's file. A link to an id no file claims
// is not an error: it is a place Patrick has named and not yet founded.
